       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Role in MG:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " pyridostigmine PO, titrated to Sx; neostigmine if parenteral tx is needed (eg, dysphagia); abdominal cramps and diarrhea → oral atropine or propantheline."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Cholinergic crisis:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " weakness from too high an anticholinesterase dose; mild crisis can mimic worse MG; severe crisis: fasciculations, ↑lacrimation and salivation, tachy, diarrhea."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Pyridostigmine bromide (Mestinon) dose, myasthenia gravis:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " Avg dose ten 60 mg tablets or ten 5 mL teaspoonfuls daily, spaced for max relief when needed. Severe cases: up to 25 tablets/teaspoonfuls a day; mild: one to six daily. TIMESPAN 180 mg: one to three tablets once or twice daily; ≥6 h between doses."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Pyridostigmine bromide (Mestinon) CI and cautions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " CI: mechanical intestinal or urinary obstruction. Use w particular caution in bronchial asthma."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Pyridostigmine bromide (Mestinon) cholinergic crisis:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " Overdosage may cause cholinergic crisis — increasing muscle weakness that may lead to death via respiratory muscle involvement. Cholinergic crisis tx: prompt withdrawal of all drugs of this type; immediate atropine also recommended. Atropine may abolish GI/muscarinic side effects, but by masking signs of overdosage can lead to inadvertent cholinergic crisis."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Pyridostigmine bromide (Mestinon) adverse reactions and renal dosing:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " SEs mostly related to overdosage: muscarinic (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal cramps, ↑ peristalsis/salivation/bronchial secretions, miosis, diaphoresis) and nicotinic (muscle cramps, fasciculation, weakness). Mainly excreted unchanged by kidney — lower doses may be required in renal disease; treatment based on titration of drug dosage to effect."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
